{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And YHWH went before them by day.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since the language of 'going' and 'transference' does not apply to the Holy One, blessed be He, Scripture explained here what it was: a pillar of cloud went before them by day to guide them on the way, and at night a pillar of fire to illuminate for them. It was a sign and token that the Name, the distinctive one, was with them day and night. And these two pillars that went with them by day and by night were for them as eyes in the way of the wilderness, and as a shield and deliverance from the Egyptians. And concerning them Scripture said (Exodus 19:4): ״ואשא אתכם על כנפי נשרים״. And since they were for Israel's deliverance and the Egyptians were judged through them — as it is written (Exodus 14:24): ״וישקף ה׳ אל מחנה מצרים בעמוד אש וענן״ — therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״והמים להם חומה״ — it is written חומה (wall) for Israel, read חמה (fury) for the Egyptians. And so they expounded concerning the host of Sennacherib (Isaiah 10:17): ״והיה אור ישראל לאש״ — light for Israel, fire for Sennacherib." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״וה׳ הולך לפניהם״ — HaShem dwelt within the pillar of cloud and went before them by day, and His court dwelt within the pillar of fire and went before them at night. And this is what they said: He and His court, and in His light are mercy and the Shekhinah. And this is (Isaiah 63:12): ״זרוע תפארתו״. And this is חומה for Israel, חמה for the Egyptians. And this is what they expounded on the verse (Exodus 15:6): ״ימינך״ — one hand of His was saving them and one hand of His was drowning them. And this is (Exodus 12:27): ״בנגפו את מצרים ואת בתינו הציל״ — for the great hand is what smites them, and the power of mercy within it is what saves. And all this is the sense of (Numbers 14:14): ״כי עין בעין נראה אתה ה׳ ועננך״ and so forth." "<b>To go by day and by night.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): because of the abundance of their desire and eagerness to receive the Torah on the fiftieth day, they would hasten their journey and go by day and by night, not like other wayfarers who tire by day and rest by night." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״ללכת יומם ולילה״ — going to receive Torah, concerning which it is written (Joshua 1:8): ״והגית בו יומם ולילה״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״ללכת יומם ולילה״ — the reason for their going by day and by night was so that they would be ready and prepared for the two attributes called יומם and לילה. And Scripture did not say 'to go by day and by night,' to teach that יומם and לילה go with them; and since this is so, Israel too needed to go after them by day and by night, and they needed to go after the pillar of cloud by day and after the pillar of fire by night. And this is the sense of ״לנחותם הדרך״, and this is the sense of ״להאיר להם״ — for this one is in the light by day and that one by night. And there was no need to write ״ללכת יומם ולילה״ except that it is an explanation of the attributes. And behold, Israel's going by day and by night after יומם and לילה is in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 24:21): ״ירא את ה׳ בני ומלך״. And on this Israel was praised in the words of the prophet, peace be upon him, who said (Jeremiah 2:2): ״זכרתי לך חסד נעוריך אהבת כלולותיך לכתך אחרי במדבר״ — after these two attributes which they loved, which are interincluded one within the other. And this is the language of כלולותיך — your interincluded ones, that is, your two attributes through which I brought you out of Egypt, which are interincluded one within the other. And this great matter, which was for Israel honor and glory in their going by day and by night for this mighty and wondrous supernal intention, was further a cause from the Master of causes, blessed be He, for Pharaoh and all Egypt — so that the Egyptians would reckon concerning Israel that they were fleeing, since they were going by day and by night, and then their heart would be strengthened and they would pursue them to the sea in order that they drown there. And blessed be HaShem, to Whom schemes were devised to destroy His rebels and to save those who fear Him."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:55.251879134Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}